    }
  },
  "Finan-17661624-XXXX": {
    "id": "Finan-17661624-XXXX",
    "name": "Higher Education Act: Eligibility and Certification",
    "category": "Financial Aid",
    "description": "To participate in Title IV financial assistance programs the university must: 1) Obtain from the Secretary certification that it provides higher education (i.e. that it is an \"eligible institution\"); and 2) where the HEA program has undergone a change in ownership that results in a change in control, the university's chief administrator and its designated HEA program administrator must complete Title IV, HEA program training within 12 months of executing the program participation agreement.",
    "version": "1.0.0",
    "statute": "1766,1624",
    "statuteReference": "Public Law No. 110-315, 20 U.S.C. § 1099c",
    "regulation": "34 C.F.R. § 668.13",
    "regulationReference": "34 C.F.R. § 668.13",
    "deadline": "Not Applicable",
    "deadlineType": "14-No Deadline",
    "validationFrequency": "asNeeded",
    "server": {
      "port": 3330,
      "running": true,
      "lastStarted": "2025-04-24T22:00:48.594Z",
      "url": "http://localhost:3330/mcp"
    }
  },
  "Finan-17661615-XXXX": {
    "id": "Finan-17661615-XXXX",
    "name": "Higher Education Act: Information Distributed to Students",
    "category": "Financial Aid",
    "description": "Annually the institution must distribute to all enrolled students a notice of availability of the following: 1) financial assistance available; 2) institutional information (e.g., cost of attendance including tuition, room, board, books and transportation; refund policy; description of academic programs and facilities; withdrawal requirements, facilities for the disabled; names of accrediting or licensing entities); 3) retention, completion, graduation, and transfer-out rates; 4) employment and graduate school enrollment information for graduates of the institution's degree programs; 5) annual security report; 6) report on athletic program participation rates and financial support data; 7) FERPA notice regarding how to obtain student information.\n\n \tThe foregoing must also be made available to prospective students upon request.\n\n \tNotice may be given online.",
    "version": "1.0.0",
    "statute": "1766,1615",
    "statuteReference": "Public Law No. 110-315, 20 U.S.C. § 1092",
    "regulation": "34 C.F.R. §§ 668.41-668.49",
    "regulationReference": "34 C.F.R. §§ 668.41-668.49",
    "deadline": "",
    "deadlineType": "9-Sep",
    "validationFrequency": "annual",
    "server": {
      "port": 3331,
      "running": true,
      "lastStarted": "2025-04-24T22:00:48.594Z",
      "url": "http://localhost:3331/mcp"
    }
  },
  "Finan-17661631-XXXX": {
    "id": "Finan-17661631-XXXX",
    "name": "Higher Education Act: Federal Supplemental Educational Opportunity Grant Program",
    "category": "Financial Aid",
    "description": "To provide Federal Supplemental Educational Opportunity Grant (FSEOG)  grants to exceptionally needy undergraduate students the university must: 1) Sign a Participation Agreement with ED; 2) maintain funds in accordance with 34 C.F.R. § 668.163; 3) maintain an internal control system so no office can both authorize and disburse payments; 4) maintain program and fiscal records; 5) contribute at least 25% of each grant; 6) limit and account for carry-forwards or carry-backs of funds; 7) follow the eligibility requirements and selection criteria set forth by ED; and 8) submit a Fiscal Operations Report and Application to Participate (FISAP) report each year.\n\n \tThe university may not transfer FSEOG funds to any other program. HEOA now allows the University to transfer 25% of FSEOG to Federal Work Study.",
    "version": "1.0.0",
    "statute": "1766,1631",
    "statuteReference": "Public Law No. 110-315, 20 U.S.C. §§ 1070b- 1070b-4",
    "regulation": "34 C.F.R. § 676.19",
    "regulationReference": "34 C.F.R. § 676.19",
    "deadline": "",
    "deadlineType": "10-Oct",
    "validationFrequency": "annual",
    "server": {
      "port": 3332,
      "running": true,
      "lastStarted": "2025-04-24T22:00:48.670Z",
      "url": "http://localhost:3332/mcp"
    }
  },
  "Finan-17661707-XXXX": {
    "id": "Finan-17661707-XXXX",
    "name": "Higher Education Act: Federal Work Study",
    "category": "Financial Aid",
    "description": "Generally, under the Federal Work Study Program (FWS), the university or the employing agency must pay 25% of the student's salary. The university must pay federal or state minimum wage, whichever is higher, for all hours worked, per the Fair Labor Standards Act. Volunteered time does not qualify.\n\n \tThe university must use 7% of FWS allocation to employ students in community service, one of whom must be a reading tutor. The university must make students aware of community service opportunities.\n\n \tPrivate, for-profit entities do not qualify as employers for community service. Students may work during non-attendance if planning to return the following semester, but if they do not do so the university must demonstrate it had reason to believe the student would returned.\n\n \tTo apply for Title III or Title V designation, and thus be eligible for a waiver of the FWS and Federal Supplemental Education Opportunity Grant (FSEOG) non-federal share requirements, an institution must complete the \"Application for Designation as an Eligible Institution\" annually through the Department of Education.",
    "version": "1.0.0",
    "statute": "1766,1707",
    "statuteReference": "Public Law No. 110-315, 42 U.S.C. §§ 2751-2757",
    "regulation": "34 C.F.R. § 675",
    "regulationReference": "34 C.F.R. § 675",
    "deadline": "",
    "deadlineType": "10-Oct",
    "validationFrequency": "annual",
    "server": {
      "port": 3333,
      "running": true,
      "lastStarted": "2025-04-24T22:00:48.669Z",
      "url": "http://localhost:3333/mcp"
    }
  },
  "Finan-16191624-XXXX": {
    "id": "Finan-16191624-XXXX",
    "name": "Higher Education Act: Financial Responsibility Requirements",
    "category": "Financial Aid",
    "description": "Provides that institutions participating in the U.S. Department of Education’s Title IV programs must be financially responsible as determined by the Secretary\n\n \tRequires institutions to notify the U.S. Department of Education within specified timeframes if certain events, actions, or conditions occur that could affect the institution’s financial responsibility",
    "version": "1.0.0",
    "statute": "1619,1624",
    "statuteReference": "20 U.S.C. § 1094, 20 U.S.C. § 1099c",
    "regulation": "34 C.F.R. Part 668",
    "regulationReference": "34 C.F.R. Part 668",
    "deadline": "",
    "deadlineType": "1-Jan",
    "validationFrequency": "annual",
    "server": {
      "port": 3334,
      "running": true,
      "lastStarted": "2025-04-24T22:00:48.670Z",
      "url": "http://localhost:3334/mcp"
    }
  },
  "Finan-16101611-XXXX": {
    "id": "Finan-16101611-XXXX",
    "name": "Higher Education Act: Net Price Calculator",
    "category": "Financial Aid",
    "description": "Any institution that receives Title IV funds must post on the institution's website a net price calculator to help current and prospective students estimate their individual net price at that institution as well as other financial aid information.\n\n \tAny institution that appears on the Department of Educationâ€™s list of institutions with the largest percentage increase in tuition and fees or net price over the most recent three academic years must submit a report to the Department that includes the reason for the increase and the steps that will be taken to reduce cost.",
    "version": "1.0.0",
    "statute": "1610,1611",
    "statuteReference": "20 U.S.C. § 1015, 20 U.S.C. § 1015a",
    "regulation": "",
    "regulationReference": "",
    "deadline": "Not Applicable",
    "deadlineType": "14-No Deadline",
    "validationFrequency": "asNeeded",
    "server": {
      "port": 3335,
      "running": true,
      "lastStarted": "2025-04-24T22:00:48.746Z",
      "url": "http://localhost:3335/mcp"
    }
  },
  "Finan-17661613-XXXX": {
    "id": "Finan-17661613-XXXX",
    "name": "Higher Education Act: PLUS Loans",
    "category": "Financial Aid",
    "description": "PLUS Loans are available to the parents of eligible students through the FDLP (Direct) or FFEL (private) program, but not both.\n\n \tIf applications are made for an FFEL PLUS loan, the University must: 1) Verify the parent's immigration status and social security number in the same manner verified for students; 2) extend PLUS loans only to parents who do not have an adverse credit history; 3) extend such loans on the same terms, conditions and benefits as other FFEL loans; 4) for any academic year, limit the loan amount to the student's estimated cost of attendance minus other financial aid; and 5) be disbursed to the institution electronically or co-payable to the school and parent.\n\n \tGraduate/professional degree students are eligible to borrow under the PLUS Loan Program up to their cost of attendance minus other estimated financial assistance in both the FFEL and Direct Loan Program. The terms and conditions applicable to Parent PLUS Loans apply to Graduate/Professional PLUS loans.",
    "version": "1.0.0",
    "statute": "1766,1613",
    "statuteReference": "Public Law No. 110-315, 20 U.S.C. § 1078-2",
    "regulation": "34 C.F.R. § 682",
    "regulationReference": "34 C.F.R. § 682",
    "deadline": "",
    "deadlineType": "9-Sep",
    "validationFrequency": "annual",
    "server": {
      "port": 3336,
      "running": true,
      "lastStarted": "2025-04-24T22:00:48.746Z",
      "url": "http://localhost:3336/mcp"
    }
  },
  "Finan-1607-XXXX": {
    "id": "Finan-1607-XXXX",
    "name": "Higher Education Act: Penalties for Drug Violations",
    "category": "Financial Aid",
    "description": "An institution must provide to each student (at time of enrollment) a written notice that advises the student that a conviction for any drug offense while receiving Title IV aid will result in a loss of eligibility for any Title IV, HEA grant, loan or work-study assistance.\n\n \tFor individuals who have lost eligibility, an institution must provide them with a written notice of Title IV eligibility loss that advises the student of the ways in which Title IV eligibility may be regained.",
    "version": "1.0.0",
    "statute": "1607",
    "statuteReference": "20 U.S.C. 1092(k)",
    "regulation": "34 C.F.R. § 668.40",
    "regulationReference": "34 C.F.R. § 668.40",
    "deadline": "",
    "deadlineType": "9-Sep",
    "validationFrequency": "annual",
    "server": {
      "port": 3337,
      "running": true,
      "lastStarted": "2025-04-24T22:00:48.746Z",
      "url": "http://localhost:3337/mcp"
    }
  },
  "Finan-17661632-XXXX": {
    "id": "Finan-17661632-XXXX",
    "name": "Higher Education Act: Perkins Loans",
    "category": "Financial Aid",
    "description": "Perkins Loans are not currently supported or funded by the federal government, and are self-sustaining only.  Authorization of additional appropriations for the Federal Perkins Loan program beyond FY2015 is prohibited.\n\n \tThe university must: 1) Make loans first to students with exceptional need; 2) provide assurances that thorough and adequate loan information is provided to student borrowers; and 3) enter into cooperative agreements with credit bureaus to exchange information concerning student borrowers.\n\n \tAt or prior to making a Perkins Loan the university must disclose thorough and adequate loan information including: 1) Name of institution of higher education and payment address; 2) principal loan amount and interest rate; 3) charges collected at or prior to disbursal and whether deducted from the loan or paid separately by borrower; 4) yearly and cumulative maximum amounts that may be borrowed; 5) when repayment is required and when accrued interest must be paid; 6) minimum and maximum repayment term, minimum monthly payment; 7) definition of default and consequences, and default penalty; 8) total cumulative balance and projected monthly payment; 9) options for consolidation or refinancing; 10) right to prepay without penalty and deferral options; 11) effect of loan on eligibility for other aid; 12) cost to borrower in making loan; and 13) a notice and explanation regarding the end to future availability of the loan made under this part.\n\n \tAn institution was able to make loans to an eligible undergraduate borrower under the Perkins program through September 30, 2016, provided that the institution had already awarded all Federal Direct Loans for which the student was eligible. Through March 31, 2018, an institution may continue to disburse Perkins Loans to an eligible graduate student to enable the student to continue or complete an academic program, provided that the student received a Perkins loan for an academic year ending prior to October 1, 2016, and provided that the institution has awarded all Federal Direct Loans for which the student is eligible.",
    "version": "1.0.0",
    "statute": "1766,1632",
    "statuteReference": "Public Law No. 110-315, 20 U.S.C. §§ 1087aa-1087ii",
    "regulation": "34 C.F.R. § 674.19",
    "regulationReference": "34 C.F.R. § 674.19",
    "deadline": "",
    "deadlineType": "10-Oct",
    "validationFrequency": "annual",
    "server": {
      "port": 3338,
      "running": true,
      "lastStarted": "2025-04-24T22:00:48.822Z",
      "url": "http://localhost:3338/mcp"
    }
  },
  "Finan-17661622-XXXX": {
    "id": "Finan-17661622-XXXX",
    "name": "Higher Education Act: Preferred Lenders",
    "category": "Financial Aid",
    "description": "The university may maintain a preferred vendor list so long as it is not used to deny or impede a borrower's choice of lenders, offers three or more lenders not affiliated with one another, and doesn't include lenders who have offered financial or other benefits to be included on the list.\n\n \tThe university must disclose to borrowers the method and criteria used to select preferred lenders, and offer comparative information to borrowers about each lender's benefits.\n\n \tThe university must include in its preferred lender materials a prominent statement that borrowers are not required to use any lender on the list, and may not delay award certification because a preferred lender is not used.\n\n \tThe preferred lender list and associated information must be updated annually.",
    "version": "1.0.0",
    "statute": "1766,1622",
    "statuteReference": "Public Law No. 110-315, 20 U.S.C. § 1094(h)",
    "regulation": "34 C.F.R. § 601.10",
    "regulationReference": "34 C.F.R. § 601.10",
    "deadline": "Not Applicable",
    "deadlineType": "14-No Deadline",
    "validationFrequency": "asNeeded",
    "server": {
      "port": 3339,
      "running": true,
      "lastStarted": "2025-04-24T22:00:48.822Z",
      "url": "http://localhost:3339/mcp"
    }
  },
  "Finan-1653-XXXX": {
    "id": "Finan-1653-XXXX",
    "name": "Qualified Tuition and Student Loan Interest Reporting",
    "category": "Financial Aid",
    "description": " Institutions must report (1) qualified tuition and related expenses and (2) student loan interest to the IRS.\n\n \tInstitutions must also provide payors with a statement that includes the tax information furnished to the IRS.",
    "version": "1.0.0",
