      "url": "http://localhost:3414/mcp"
    }
  },
  "Sexua-1753-XXXX": {
    "id": "Sexua-1753-XXXX",
    "name": "Campus Sex Crimes Prevention Act (§ 1601 of the Victims of Trafficking and Violence Protection Act of 2000)",
    "category": "Sexual Misconduct",
    "description": "The Campus Sex Crimes Prevention Act requires sex offenders, who must register under state law, to provide notice of enrollment or employment at any institution of higher education (IHE) in that state where the offender resides, as well as notice of each change of enrollment or employment status at the IHE. In turn, this information will be made available by the state authorities to the local law enforcement agency that has jurisdiction where the IHE is located.",
    "version": "1.0.0",
    "statute": "1753",
    "statuteReference": "Public Law No. 106-386 (Title VI)",
    "regulation": "34 C.F.R. § 668",
    "regulationReference": "34 C.F.R. § 668",
    "deadline": "",
    "deadlineType": "10-Oct",
    "validationFrequency": "annual",
    "server": {
      "port": 3415,
      "running": true,
      "lastStarted": "2025-04-24T22:00:50.723Z",
      "url": "http://localhost:3415/mcp"
    }
  },
  "Sexua-16164202-XXXX": {
    "id": "Sexua-16164202-XXXX",
    "name": "Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) and Violence Against Women Act (VAWA)",
    "category": "Sexual Misconduct",
    "description": "Any institution that participates in federal financial aid programs must collect information with respect to campus crime statistics and campus security policies of the institution. The institution must annually distribute to current students, employees, and (upon request) prospective students or employees, an annual security report (ASR) containing various statements of institutional policies, descriptions of programs, and campus crime statistics.\n\n \tUnder the Violence Against Women Act Reauthorization Act of 2022, the Secretary of Education is required to develop a national survey “to gather information on student experiences with domestic violence, dating violence, sexual assault, sexual harassment, and stalking” be reported in a biennial report and online as a part of the Department of Education’s online consumer information regarding specific institutions. Each institution receiving federal assistance must administer the survey and publish on its website “campus-level results of the standardized elements” of the survey.",
    "version": "1.0.0",
    "statute": "1616,4202",
    "statuteReference": "20 U.S.C. § 1092(f), 20 U.S.C. § 1161l–6",
    "regulation": "34 C.F.R. § 668.41(e)",
    "regulationReference": "34 C.F.R. § 668.41(e), 34 C.F.R. § 668.46",
    "deadline": "",
    "deadlineType": "10-Oct",
    "validationFrequency": "annual",
    "server": {
      "port": 3416,
      "running": true,
      "lastStarted": "2025-04-24T22:00:50.799Z",
      "url": "http://localhost:3416/mcp"
    }
  },
  "Sexua-1633-XXXX": {
    "id": "Sexua-1633-XXXX",
    "name": "Title IX of the Education Amendment of 1972",
    "category": "Sexual Misconduct",
    "description": "Prohibits discrimination on the basis of sex in education programs or activities receiving federal financial assistance including employment.\n\n \tTitle IX protects students from sexual harassment in educational programs or activities operated by recipients of federal funding. The protection against sexual harassment derives from the general prohibitions against sex discrimination contained in the Title IX common rule.\n\n \tED regulations define sexual harassment, including sexual assault, as unlawful sex discrimination and set forth obligations colleges and universities must meet when responding to reports of sexual harassment.\n\n \tAny grievance records relating to a Title IX violation or complaint must be retained for the period of time cited in state law for personal injury actions. Under ED regulations governing sexual harassment, grievance records relating to a Title IX violation or complaint must be retained for seven years.\n\n \t<li style=\"list-style-type: none;\">",
    "version": "1.0.0",
    "statute": "1633",
    "statuteReference": "20 U.S.C. §§ 1681-1688",
    "regulation": "28 C.F.R. §§ 42.201-42.215",
    "regulationReference": "28 C.F.R. §§ 42.201-42.215, 34 C.F.R. § 106, 85 Fed. Reg. 30,026, 29 C.F.R. § 1604, 45 C.F.R. § 86",
    "deadline": "Not Applicable",
    "deadlineType": "14-No Deadline",
    "validationFrequency": "asNeeded",
    "server": {
      "port": 3417,
      "running": true,
      "lastStarted": "2025-04-24T22:00:50.799Z",
      "url": "http://localhost:3417/mcp"
    }
  },
  "Sexua-1706-XXXX": {
    "id": "Sexua-1706-XXXX",
    "name": "Title VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964",
    "category": "Sexual Misconduct",
    "description": "Title VII prohibits discrimination in hiring, firing, training, promotion, discipline, or other workplace decisions on the basis of an employee or applicant's race, color, sex, national origin, or religion.\n\n \tSexual harassment is also prohibited under this law.",
    "version": "1.0.0",
    "statute": "1706",
    "statuteReference": "42 U.S.C. §§ 2000e-2000e-17",
